<h1>Drag Chain &amp; Tubular Drag Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A drag chain conveyor uses endless chains with attached flights, paddles, or discs to pull bulk material through an enclosed trough or tube. Drag chain conveyors handle dry bulk materials, grain, flour, cement, chemical powders, plastic pellets, in totally enclosed paths that contain dust and prevent contamination. The three main types are en-masse drag (rectangular trough, high capacity), tubular drag (sealed tube, gentle handling), and chain-in-tube (hybrid designs for specialty materials).</p>
<h2 id="enmasse-heading">En-Masse Drag Conveyors</h2>
<p>En-masse drag conveyors move bulk material as a continuous column within a rectangular steel trough. The chain and flights occupy only a small part of the trough cross-section. The rest is filled with material that moves as a slug. Internal friction between particles transmits the drag force from the flights throughout the material column, carrying the bulk load with lower chain tension than conventional scraper conveyors.</p>
<h3>Operating Principle</h3>
<p>The en-masse principle works because cohesive bulk materials develop internal shear strength. The flights push against the material immediately in front of them. That material pushes the material ahead, and so on, creating a moving column. The trough walls contain the column. The result: the chain carries only the drag force, not the material weight. This reduces chain wear, power consumption, and component stress compared to flight conveyors where each flight supports the full material weight above it.</p>
<h3>Capacity and Performance</h3>
<p>En-masse capacity depends on trough cross-section, chain speed, and material bulk density. Large units move very high tonnage: <a href="https://www.metso.com/portfolio/conveyors/en-masse-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Metso rates its en-masse conveyors</a> at over 600 tons per hour in a single machine and up to 900°F material temperature. Chain speeds are slow compared with belt conveyors, which limits wear and keeps the material column intact. The enclosed trough provides dust containment and keeps external contaminants out.</p>
<h3>Path Configurations</h3>
<p>En-masse conveyors operate in horizontal, inclined, and combination paths, and some designs also run vertically. The allowable incline depends on the material and the flight design, so confirm it with the manufacturer's material test. A single en-masse conveyor can run horizontal, transition to an incline, and return to horizontal, eliminating transfer points between separate horizontal and inclined conveyors. Multiple inlets along the trough accept material from different sources. Multiple discharge gates along the trough deliver material to different destinations.</p>
<p>Food-grade versions use stainless steel troughs and food-contact-compliant chain and flight materials for grain, flour, sugar, and animal feed.</p>
<h2 id="tubular-heading">Tubular Drag Conveyors</h2>
<p>Tubular drag conveyors use a chain with evenly spaced circular discs (flights) that travel through a sealed tube. Each disc fits closely within the tube bore. Standard tube sizes are small: <a href="https://www.mpechicago.com/chain-vey/layouts-and-designs/chain-drag-conveyor-capacities/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chain-Vey offers 3 to 8 inch tubes</a>, for example. Material fills the space between consecutive discs and moves as a plug as the chain advances. The sealed tube provides complete dust containment and material protection.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/drag-mpe-chain-vey-tubular.webp" width="780" height="519" alt="Tubular conveyor lines with clear sections run up from process equipment and curve along a timber ceiling, with evenly spaced discs visible inside the tubes." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A tubular drag conveyor installation with clear tube sections. The evenly spaced discs are visible inside the tubes as the lines climb and turn overhead. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.mpechicago.com/chain-vey/layouts-and-designs/chain-drag-conveyor-capacities/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Modern Process Equipment</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Gentle Material Handling</h3>
<p>Tubular drag conveyors move material at low speed with minimal agitation. The material sits between discs without tumbling, dropping, or air exposure. This gentle handling preserves fragile materials: whole nuts, snack foods, breakfast cereals, pet food kibble, coffee beans, and pharmaceutical tablets. Material degradation (broken pieces, fines generation, dust creation) is generally lower than <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/screw-conveyors/">screw conveyors</a>, or pneumatic systems.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/drag-cablevey-clear-tube.webp" width="1000" height="668" alt="Two clear vertical conveyor tubes: one holds ring cereal in separate pockets between discs, the other holds small piles of powder on each disc." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Clear tubes on a tubular drag conveyor carrying cereal and powder. Each disc carries its own pocket of material, so the product moves without tumbling. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://cablevey.com/what-are-tubular-drag-conveyors-and-how-do-they-work/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cablevey Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>3D Routing Flexibility</h3>
<p>Tubular drag conveyors route through horizontal runs, vertical lifts, and sweeping curves within a single continuous chain loop. A single tubular drag system can pick up material at floor level, lift it to a mezzanine, run horizontally to a processing point, and discharge, all without transfer points. This 3D routing eliminates the multiple conveyors and transfer points that conventional systems require for complex paths.</p>
<p>Tubular drag systems come in two drive styles. Chain-based systems use stainless or carbon steel roller or link chain with attached discs. Cable-based systems use a coated steel cable instead: <a href="https://cablevey.com/what-are-tubular-drag-conveyors-and-how-do-they-work/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cablevey describes its cable</a> as a stainless steel wire strand core cable sealed in a nylon jacket, with discs attached along the loop.</p>
<h3>Capacity Limitations</h3>
<p>Tubular drag conveyors handle lower capacities than en-masse systems. Chain-Vey lists a maximum of 175 cubic feet per hour for its 3 inch tube and 2,000 cubic feet per hour for its 8 inch tube. Cablevey cites a maximum of about 80,000 lb per hour for dry food products. Actual tonnage depends on bulk density. The small tube that provides gentle handling also limits throughput, so large-volume applications need parallel tubular systems or a switch to en-masse drag or belt conveyors.</p>
<h2 id="chainintube-heading">Chain-in-Tube Systems</h2>
<p>Chain-in-tube conveyors are a hybrid category that includes any drag conveyor using a chain within a tubular housing. This encompasses both tubular drag conveyors (disc-type) and specialized designs with scraper blades, paddles, or bucket attachments within tubes. The tube provides total enclosure while the chain type and attachment style vary by application.</p>
<h3>Scraper Chain-in-Tube</h3>
<p>Scraper-type chain-in-tube conveyors use flat blades or paddles attached to the chain that scrape material along the tube bottom. They handle materials too heavy or too sticky for disc-type tubular drag: wet grain, damp sawdust, and semi-dry sludge. The scraper action generates more friction and material degradation than disc-type systems but handles a wider range of material conditions.</p>
<h3>Bucket Chain-in-Tube</h3>
<p>Some chain-in-tube systems use small buckets attached to the chain within a tube. These combine the total enclosure of tubular systems with the carrying capacity and clean discharge of bucket systems. They handle materials that flow poorly (bridging, ratholing) and need positive containment during vertical lifts. This design overlaps with <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevator</a> functionality but in a tubular housing.</p>
