<h2 id="apps-heading">Drag Chain Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Grain and feed handling:</strong> En-masse drag conveyors transfer grain between storage bins, dryers, cleaners, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging equipment</a>. The enclosed trough prevents grain contamination and dust emission. Multiple inlet and discharge gates along the trough serve multiple bins from a single conveyor run. Feed mills use drag chain conveyors to move ingredients between mixers, pelletizers, and bagging stations.</p>
<p><strong>Snack food and cereal:</strong> Tubular drag conveyors move fragile finished products, chips, crackers, cereals, nuts, from processing to packaging with minimal breakage. The gentle plug-flow movement preserves product integrity. A single tubular drag system replaces multiple belt conveyors and bucket elevators that would break fragile products at each transfer point.</p>
<p><strong>Chemical powder transfer:</strong> En-masse and tubular drag conveyors move chemical powders, catalysts, and additives between reactors, dryers, classifiers, and packaging. The enclosed design prevents operator exposure to hazardous dusts and prevents product contamination. Stainless steel construction handles corrosive chemicals. Explosion-proof drives and inerting systems address <a href="https://www.osha.gov/combustible-dust" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">combustible dust hazards</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Cement and mineral processing:</strong> Heavy-duty en-masse drag conveyors move cement, fly ash, calcium carbonate, and mineral powders at high capacity. The <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">chain conveyor</a> construction handles abrasive materials better than belt conveyors in enclosed applications. Drag chain conveyors feed <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a>, silos, and packaging lines.</p>
<p><strong>Pet food manufacturing:</strong> Tubular drag conveyors handle finished pet food kibble from extruders and coaters to packaging. The gentle handling preserves kibble shape and coating integrity. The sealed tube prevents cross-contamination between flavors and recipes. Quick-clean features (split tube sections, removable discs) facilitate sanitation between product changeovers.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical ingredients:</strong> Tubular drag conveyors in stainless steel construction move pharmaceutical powders, granules, and tablets in contained environments. The sealed tube meets containment requirements for active pharmaceutical ingredients (APIs). Clean-in-place (CIP) capability allows thorough cleaning between batches. The gentle handling preserves tablet coatings and reduces fines generation.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selection &amp; Maintenance</h2>
<p><strong>Capacity determines the type.</strong> Moderate rates with fragile material point to tubular drag. High tonnage points to en-masse drag. The crossover depends on material bulk density and allowable degradation levels. High-value fragile products justify the lower capacity of tubular drag. Commodity bulk materials use higher-capacity en-masse systems.</p>
<p><strong>Material characteristics define the chain and trough.</strong> Abrasive materials need hardened chain pins, hardened flight edges, and AR (abrasion-resistant) plate troughs. Corrosive materials need stainless steel chain and trough. High-temperature materials need alloy steel chain and a temperature rating from the manufacturer. Sticky materials need flights with UHMW faces or Teflon coatings to prevent buildup.</p>
<p><strong>Chain tension is the critical maintenance parameter.</strong> Over-tensioned chain accelerates pin and bushing wear. Under-tensioned chain sags, catches on trough edges, and increases wear. Monitor chain elongation as an indicator of wear and replace at the chain maker's limit. <a href="https://www.ustsubaki.com/resources/knowledge-center/chain-wear-indicator-kit/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tsubaki</a> treats 1.5% to 2% as the typical wear life limit for industrial roller chain, while <a href="https://www.reliableplant.com/Read/8966/wear-life-roller-chains" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Machinery Lubrication</a> puts the maximum at about 3% for most applications and about 1.5% for fixed-center drives. Automatic take-up mechanisms (screw, hydraulic, or gravity) maintain proper tension between adjustments.</p>
<p><strong>Cleanout and changeover frequency matter.</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food</a> and pharmaceutical applications require frequent cleanout between products. Tubular drag systems with split tubes, quick-disconnect fittings, and CIP capability clean faster than en-masse troughs with bolted covers. Factor cleanout time and frequency into system selection. When changeovers are frequent, a tubular system that cleans much faster than a bolted-cover trough can justify its higher capital cost.</p>
<p><strong>Compared to alternatives:</strong> Drag chain conveyors compete with <a href="/conveyor-types/screw-conveyors/">screw conveyors</a> (simpler, cheaper, but more material degradation and limited path flexibility), pneumatic conveyors (highest flexibility but highest energy cost and most material degradation), and belt conveyors (highest capacity but not enclosed). Drag chain provides the best balance of enclosure, material protection, and path flexibility for moderate-capacity applications.</p>
