<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a drag chain conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A drag chain conveyor pulls bulk material through an enclosed trough or tube using chains with flights or discs. Types include en-masse (high capacity), tubular drag (gentle handling), and chain-in-tube (hybrid). See our <a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">chain conveyor hub</a> for all chain-based systems.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">En-masse vs tubular drag, which is better?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>En-masse for high-capacity commodity materials. Tubular drag for fragile materials at moderate rates that need gentle handling and 3D routing. En-masse usually costs less per ton of capacity. Tubular drag preserves product quality and provides total dust containment in a sealed tube.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What materials can a drag chain conveyor handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Most dry and semi-dry bulk materials: grain, flour, sugar, cement, chemical powders, plastic pellets, pet food, coffee beans, pharmaceutical powders. Not suitable for highly abrasive, very sticky/wet, or very large particle materials. Material testing by the manufacturer determines suitability.</p>

</div>
